===Noun===
{{en-noun|p}}

# {{senseid|en|distant sky}}{{lb|en|often with 'the'}} The distant sky of the sun, moon, and stars.
#* {{RQ:Shakespeare Comedy of Errors|I|i|66|2|For what obſcured light the '''heauens''' did grant,{{nb...}}}}
#* {{quote-book|en|year=1625|author=Nathanæl Carpenter|title=Geography delineated forth in two bookes|section=I iv 77
|passage={{...}}'''the ''Heauens''''', which are carryed in 24 [[hours|houres]] from Eaſt to Weſt,{{nb...}}}}
#* {{quote-journal|en|year=1930|month=March|work=Nature|volume=179|issue=2|passage=The moon's path lies in that belt of '''the heavens''' known as the zodiac.}}
#* {{quote-book|en|year=1981|author=E.R. Harrison|title=Cosmology|section=XII 250
|passage={{...}}in an infinitely large universe the stars would collectively outshine the Sun and flood '''the heavens''' with light far more intense than is observed.}}
# {{lb|en|religion}} {{plural of|en|heaven}}: the abode of God or the gods; the abode of the blessed departed.
#* {{quote-book|en|year=1832|author=Charles Coleman|title=The Mythology of the Hindus|section=XIII 220
|passage=Like the Buddhas, they <nowiki>[</nowiki>''the [[Jain]]s''<nowiki>]</nowiki> believe that there is a plurality of '''heavens''' and hells;}}
#* {{quote-journal|en|date=July 30 1906|journal=Washington Post|section=12 4
|passage=Christ's coming from the '''heavens''' has entered into the life of humanity as the Founder of the world to come.}}
# {{lb|en|rare}} {{plural of|en|heaven}}: the near sky of the weather, etc.
# {{lb|en|rare}} {{plural of|en|heaven}} the will of God or the gods, [[Providence]].
#* {{RQ:King James Version|Daniel|4|26|page=947|column=1|text={{...}}after that thou ſhalt [[have|haue]] [[known|knowen]] that '''the heauens''' [[do]]e rule.}}

====Usage notes====
*The plural form "heavens" or "the heavens" has been typical in reference to the impersonal sky since the 17th century. The plural form became less common in reference to the abode of God, the gods, or the blessed departed as the medieval view of [[celestial sphere]]s was disproven; it is still commonly used, however, in discussing theologies such as [[Buddhism]] which retain numerous heavens or levels of heaven.
* The term is capitalized as '''[[Heavens]]''' or "the Heavens" when (infrequently) used as a proper name.

